## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to handle equipment with strong reflective/transparent surfaces, a realistic industrial setting, and on-device adaptation under tight latency budgets.

## Key Concepts
- Reflective/transparent object pose estimation
- Differentiable rendering for photometric consistency
- On-device 3-shot adaptation via compact adapters
- Synthetic+real data with material randomization
- Edge latency and robustness under glare

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch
function forward(frame) {
  const feats = backbone(frame);
  const seg = segHead(feats);
  const pose = poseHead(feats);
  const render = differentiableRenderer(pose, frame);
  return {seg, pose, loss: computeLoss(render, frame)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to varying refractive indices?
- How would you quantify uncertainty in pose under specular highlights?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a practical monocular 6-DoF pose estimator that handles symmetry, occlusion, and online adaptation in a real-world manufacturing setting. Emphasizes differentiable refinement, data strategies, and latency planning.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular 6-DoF pose estimation
- Differentiable rendering for pose refinement
- Symmetry handling and occlusion robustness
- Synthetic data + domain randomization
- Online adaptation with few-shot updates
- Real-time latency targets (embedded GPU)

## Code Example
```javascript
class PoseHead {
  constructor(featDim) {
    this.fcRot = linear(featDim, 4); // quaternion
    this.fcTrans = linear(featDim, 3);
  }
  forward(feat) {
    const q = normalize(this.fcRot(feat), 2);
    const t = this.fcTrans(feat);
    return { quat: q, trans: t };
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate symmetry handling for objects with known symmetry? 
- How would you mitigate catastrophic forgetting during online adaptation?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design cross-modal, real-time computer vision pipelines that integrate on-device adaptation with differentiable geometry for robotic manipulation and planning in unstructured environments.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time monocular depth and surface normals estimation from a single RGB camera
- Differentiable probabilistic mesh prediction integrated with motion planning
- Few-shot (5-shot) on-device adaptation using synthetic pre-training followed by real-world fine-tuning
- Multi-task learning with shared encoders and task-specific heads

## Code Example

```javascript
// Core inference pipeline sketch
function forwardPass(frame) {
  const features = sharedEncoder(frame);
  const depth = depthHead(features);
  const normals = normalHead(features);
  const mesh = meshHead(features);
  return { depth, normals, mesh };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you quantify uncertainty in the mesh predictions to ensure safe manipulation?
- What strategies would you use to handle reflective or texture-less surfaces where depth estimation typically fails?
- How does the system handle dynamic scene changes between adaptation and deployment?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a constrained, real-time CV system that reasons about 3D pose, occlusion, and actionable outcomes (feasibility of mating for tool swaps) in an edge context with new hardware revisions.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular 6-DoF pose for multiple components
- On-device adaptation (3-shot) to unseen revisions
- Occlusion-robust representation (2.5D priors)
- Tool-mating feasibility as a discriminative objective
- Synthetic+real data strategy and edge latency trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: pose and feasibility total loss
function totalLoss(posePred, poseGT, feasPred, feasGT, mask) {
  const poseDiff = posePred.sub(poseGT).mul(mask);
  const poseLoss = poseDiff.pow(2).sum() / mask.sum();
  const feasLoss = binaryCrossEntropy(feasPred, feasGT, mask);
  return poseLoss * 0.7 + feasLoss * 0.3;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would temporal consistency be enforced across frames on device?
- Which ablations show the impact of 2.5D priors vs full 3D reasoning on occlusion-heavy scenes?
